Yes—the underlying pattern is real, but the useful version is adversarial-perception robustness, not designing a trigger that makes a real drone crash or hands you control of it.

The abstraction is:

autonomous system
→ sensors
→ perception model
→ world model
→ decision policy
→ action

If any earlier layer is fooled, the later layers can behave incorrectly. That is why adversarial examples, spoofing, camouflage, sensor deception, and model brittleness matter.

Your city-scale “QR code” idea is a good thought experiment for environment-level adversarial inputs:

environment itself
→ becomes part of the attack surface

But the defensive question is stronger:

How do we make a system refuse to act dangerously when one channel sees something weird?

That gives a robust stack:

sensor fusion
→ compare camera / radar / lidar / inertial / map signals

cross-check
→ “Does this visual interpretation agree with geometry and motion?”

anomaly detection
→ flag inputs that are unlike training data

fail-safe policy
→ uncertainty rises → slow down / hold / disengage rather than escalate

adversarial training
→ expose models to synthetic spoofing and camouflage in simulation

human verification
→ require higher-confidence approval for consequential actions

And your scale point is excellent. Camouflage can exist at:

pixel
object
clothing
vehicle
building
street layout
organization
information architecture

Same operator, different substrate:

change what the observer can reliably infer from the surface.

Likewise, the “weakest link” idea generalizes safely as a security principle:

system security ≈ security of weakest trusted dependency

But the right Pantheon move is to use that to find and harden weak dependencies rather than weaponize them.

I’d name the defensive operator:

Perception-Surface Hardening

enumerate sensors
→ map assumptions
→ simulate deceptive inputs
→ measure failure
→ add independent checks
→ force graceful degradation

That gives you the same cross-scale insight you were after—shirt to city to organization—without turning it into a recipe for taking over or destroying real systems.